// GRADUATED FROM THE SPECIMEN SLOT (silent-class-inventory D311, closed 2026-08-28). Program 1
// is verbatim the program `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts`'s `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` carried
// between #1988 and this close, and the file that held it was
// `tests/cases/soundness/xfail-miscompile-adopted-string-atom-print-route.vl`,
// `@no-instantiate`, deleted in the same commit. On the merge base (seed 1,464,975) this whole
// file is ONE module that does not validate — `type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)`.
//
// THE MECHANISM — the fourth end of D209's channel, and the first three were all working.
// `structIndexOfObjCtx` adopts the declared row, the store boxes `"7"` under the string tag,
// the channel predicate fires (`reach=2 ans=2`), the read delivers a bare `(ref $sTypeIdx)` and
// `exprUnion` correctly stops calling it a union value. What was wrong is one step later: the
// two READ classifiers `exprUnion` left behind still answered "this is a BOX" about a value the
// read had already unboxed. `memberUnionReadKind` answered -2, so `exprString` was false and
// `print` fell past every string arm to `__print_i32__`; and `unionNameOfExpr` still named the
// union, so a binding over such a read was REGISTERED as a union binding and
// `unionIdentReadKind` overrode `exprString` at every later read of it. Both now ask the same
// predicate the read site and `exprUnion` ask — three classifiers, one decision.
//
// WHY ONLY THE STRING ATOM (programs 7 and 8 are the measurement, not decoration). The other
// four consumer classifiers `print` dispatches on — `exprIsF32` / `exprIsF64` / `exprIsI64` /
// `exprIsBool` — each open with a typed-IR fast path off the CHECKER's type for the node,
// which is exactly the atom this channel delivers, so they were already right. A Member arm on
// each of the four was BUILT as a counter probe and scored `ans=0` at 31,062 corpus reaches
// and 623 grid reaches, so none of them is written.
// 1 — THE SPECIMEN, verbatim. The slot's `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` between #1988 and this close.
type C1 = { r1: string | i32 }
const v1 = { r1: "7" }
print((v1).r1)

// 2 — the same read through a LIST receiver, which is the coordinate the narrow cannot key
// (`memberPathKeyOf` answers "" for an Index receiver, so nothing installs a member path).
type C2 = { r2: string | i32 }
const lv2 = [{ r2: "7" }]
print((lv2[0]).r2)

// 3 — the LOCAL rebind. The binding's SLOT was already a string ref (`letIsString` reads the
// init through `exprString`), and the print still routed to `__print_i32__`, because the
// binding had ALSO been registered as a union NAME — rung N is what this program needs.
type C3 = { r3: string | i32 }
const v3 = { r3: "7" }
const z3 = (v3).r3
print(z3)

// 4 — the local rebind through the list receiver.
type C4 = { r4: string | i32 }
const lv4 = [{ r4: "7" }]
const z4 = (lv4[0]).r4
print(z4)

// 5 — the `is` GUARD over a list receiver. The guard installs nothing (no path key), so the
// read inside it is the un-narrowed one and takes the channel, not `emitUnionFieldNarrowUnbox`.
type C5 = { r5: string | i32 }
const lv5 = [{ r5: "7" }]
if (lv5[0]).r5 is string { print((lv5[0]).r5) } else { print(0) }

// 6 — THE CONTROL THAT ALREADY RAN, and the axis pair that separates it: a BARE receiver's
// guard DOES install a member-path binding, so `narrowedValueAtomOf` answers and the channel
// is never asked (`S reach=0` on this program). It must keep running.
type C6 = { r6: string | i32 }
const v6 = { r6: "7" }
if (v6).r6 is string { print((v6).r6) } else { print(0) }

// 7 — THE i32 SIDE OF THE SAME FIELD, which always ran: the read delivers the i32 atom and
// `print`'s numeric default is right by accident. It is the reason this family was silent at
// exactly one atom rather than at all of them.
type C7 = { r7: i32 | string }
const v7 = { r7: 7 }
print((v7).r7)

// 8 — AND THE f64 ATOM, which also always ran and by a DIFFERENT route: `exprIsF64` opens with
// a typed-IR fast path off the checker's own type for the node, which is exactly the atom this
// channel delivers. `exprString` has no such fast path on purpose (canon softens a literal
// union to the spelling `string`), which is why the string atom — and only it — was wrong.
type C8 = { r8: f64 | string }
const v8 = { r8: 7.0 }
print((v8).r8)
